
  [34mconst[39m ENVIRONMENTS = {
    [36mdevelopment[39m: { [36mapiBaseUrl[39m: [31m"http://localhost:3000"[39m },
    [36mstaging[39m:     { [36mapiBaseUrl[39m: [31m"https://staging-api.example.com"[39m },
    [36mproduction[39m:  { [36mapiBaseUrl[39m: [31m"https://api.example.com"[39m },
  };

  [38;5;153mdetectEnvironment()[39m reads [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m and returns an environment name:

  - [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m, [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m, or [38;5;153m""[39m (an [38;5;153mindex.html[39m opened directly via [38;5;153mfile://[39m) → [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m
  - hostname beginning [38;5;153mstaging.[39m → [38;5;153mstaging[39m
  - anything else → [38;5;153mproduction[39m

  It then publishes [38;5;153mwindow.AppConfig = { environment, apiBaseUrl }[39m — a resolved object, not the whole table, so callers
  can't accidentally reach into another environment.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m gains [38;5;153m<script src="config.js"></script>[39m immediately before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag. Ordering is what
  guarantees [38;5;153mAppConfig[39m exists; plain scripts execute in document order, so this is deterministic.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[0m loses the [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m constant. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m composes its URL from [38;5;153mwindow.AppConfig.apiBaseUrl + "/login"[39m at call
  time rather than at load time — same result today, but it won't silently bake in a stale value if the config is ever
  set later.

  [1mTwo behavioral decisions worth your explicit sign-off[0m, because both are judgment calls rather than consequences of
  what you already chose:

  1. [1mUnknown hostnames resolve to production.[0m That follows from "everything else → production", but it's worth naming
     the risk: a new deploy target you forget to add will quietly talk to the prod API rather than failing loudly. The
     alternative is throwing on an unrecognized host, which is safer but breaks any host not in the list.
  2. [1mA missing [38;5;153mAppConfig[39m throws a clear error.[0m If [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m fails to load, I'd rather [38;5;153mlogin()[39m raise [38;5;153m"AppConfig not [39m
     [38;5;153mloaded"[39m than POST to [38;5;153mundefined/login[39m, which fails confusingly much later.

  Does that structure look right before I cover testing?

